<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Package visibility (Android 11+) for the wallpaper section's **installed live wallpapers** shelf, which asks the
      package manager for every service answering `WallpaperService`. Without this declaration that query is filtered
      down to services in *this* app — i.e. it returns only our own `RotatingWallpaperService`, which the shelf then
      deliberately excludes, so the row renders permanently empty and looks like a device with no live wallpapers
      installed rather than a query that was never allowed to see them.

      Declared here, beside the code that runs the query, for the same reason `data:apps` declares its launcher query
      and `data:wallpaper` declares `SET_WALLPAPER`: the capability travels with the module that needs it. And
      deliberately narrow rather than L1's `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES`, which asked for visibility of every package on the
      device to answer this one question.

      Not covered by the launcher role either: being the default home app exempts `LauncherApps` from visibility
      filtering, not `PackageManager.queryIntentServices`.
    -->
    <!--
      And package visibility for the **home app itself**, which `DefaultLauncherRole.isDefault` resolves to answer
      whether that is us. Without this the answer is not merely missing, it is *inverted*, which is why it earns its
      own note beside the one above: a launcher is one of the few kinds of app that does **not** declare
      MAIN/LAUNCHER — it would then sit in its own app drawer — so the query `data:apps` already declares cannot see
      Pixel Launcher, Nova, or any other. The resolution is filtered down to the only HOME activity we are allowed to
      see, which is *our own*, so a launcher that is not the default concludes that it is, and the row offering to
      make it the default is never drawn. Nothing errors and nothing is empty; the screen is simply wrong.

      Narrow, like its neighbor: this exposes only activities answering MAIN/HOME, which on a device is a handful.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.service.wallpaper.WallpaperService" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

</manifest>
